Enhanced methane production by bimetallic metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as cathode in an anaerobic digestion microbial electrolysis cell
Zheng Xiaomei, Lin Rujing, Xu Jun, He Yingying, Zhang Xinying, Xie Li
Key Laboratory of Yangtze River Water Environment, Ministry of Education, Tongji University, College of Environmental Science and Engineering, Tongji University, Shanghai 200092, China
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: Ni/Co-NC cathode Microbial electrolysis cell \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) biomethanation Hydrogenotrophic methanogenesis Direct electron transfer
Cathode materials play important roles in the optimization of methane upgrading in microbial electrolysis cell-coupled anaerobic digestion (MEC-AD) systems. In this study, metal organic framework (MOF)-derived carbon-based bimetallic hybrids (Ni/Co-NC) was synthesized as cathode material and its performances and microbial activities were evaluated in MEC-AD. A high methane production rate \((0.57\,\mathrm{~m}^{3}\,\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{m}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{d})\) and yield \((0.34\;\mathrm{m}^{3}\) \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{COD})\) were achieved with a Ni/Co-NC cathode at a potential of \(0.6\:\mathrm{V}\) . The methane content in the biogas was upgraded to ca. \(90\%\) . The enhanced methane production was likely related to the positive surface charge and the good conductive capacity and hydrogen evolution of the Ni/Co-NC cathode, as demonstrated by electrochemical assessment. Additionally, roughness and extensive surface areas provided additional room for cathodic biofilm adhesion via hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions. A significant enrichment of Methanobacterium \((79.6\%)\) in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC system, especially in the biofilm of the cathode surface, indicated that the methane production pathway shifted from acetoclastic to hydrogenotrophic methanogenesis. Moreover, Methanosaeta harudinacea 6Ac species, which can directly accept electrons, were identified in the MEC-AD systems. These findings revealed that Ni/Co-NC cathode probably enhanced bioelectrochemical reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) by accelerating hydrogenotrophic methanogenesis and direct electron transfer.
## 1. Introduction
With the depletion of fossil fuel resource, environmental awareness and energy shortages have become the focus of global attention. Biogas produced from the anaerobic digestion (AD) of organic wastes composed of methane \((50\sim70\%)\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \((30\sim50\%)\) [1]. As a renewable bioenergy source, biogas has the potential to replace fossil fuel. However, the long cycles of AD processes and the high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) content in the biogas reduce the methane production rate and quality. Recently, a novel combined technology, microbial electrolysis cell-coupled AD (MEC-AD), has been proposed to accelerate methane production with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) fixation and conversion [2]. MEC provides a microbial and electrochemical reaction platform with a hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at the cathode and an electron acceptor at the anode, so methane can be produced via three pathways (pathway I: \(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H+H_{2}O\rightarrow C H_{4}+H_{2}C O_{3}}\) , \(\Delta\mathbf{G}^{0}=\) \(-31\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) , pathway II: \(2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}\) , \(4\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{CO}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{4}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) \(\Delta\mathrm{G}_{0}^{\prime}=-131\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) , pathway III: \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}+8\mathrm{e}^{-}+8\mathrm{H}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{4}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) \(\Delta\mathrm{G}_{0}^{\prime}=-136\;\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) [3].
As an electron reservoir, the cathode plays a dual role in the microbe
electrode interactions and in the extracellular electron transfers required for the autotrophic reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to methane in MEC-AD. Among the different operating and physical parameters, the cathode material is one of the most important factors determining the performance of MEC-AD, especially during a biogas upgrading process [4]. Generally, platinum (Pt)-catalysed cathodes (Pt-coated carbon cloths, carbon black, etc.) have been widely applied in MEC-AD systems for electromethanogenesis due to their low overpotential, excellent catalytic and commercial availability [5-7]. Researchers have also modified cathode with conductive nanoparticles or functional groups, such as Ni, Fe, magnetite/zeolite nanocomposites, polyaniline, and polypyrrole, have been tested for MEC-AD to enhance electron transfer from the cathode electrodes to microorganisms [8,9]. However, their high cost, security, easy poisoning by sulfide and inefficient interactions with microorganisms lowered the performance of MEC-AD systems from optimum levels [10]. So it is of practical significance to develop superior activity, low-cost and stability cathode for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) methanation [4].
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of porous, functional, three-dimensional crystal materials that self-assemble with inorganic
metal ions and organic ligands [11], which have been extensively applied for gas adsorption [12], \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction [13], and hydrogen evolution reactions [14]. Currently, MOFs are popular as electrode catalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction due to their outstanding features, such as large specific surface area, structural modularity, diverse chemical compositions and high porosity [15,16]. Nevertheless, the inferior electroconductivity of the monometallic MOF materials is still the bottleneck in applications of electrocatalysis. An efficient strategy is to package transition metals with MOF-derived carbon matrix composites, which can provide accessible active sites and ultimately improve the material's electrochemical performance [17]. Recently, several notable studies have been conducted to enhance the activity of electrode materials. For example, nickel-containing polyoxometalates were encapsulated into MOFs such as MIL-100 (Fe) and UIO-67 [18]. Moreover, electrode catalysts integrating MOFs with transition metal materials (Co, Ni and Mn, etc.) were shown to be conducive to more efficient electron/ion diffusion on the surface [19]. The active layer of the catalysts on the cathode can also play a pivotal role in the surface-driven electron uptake by \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-reducing bacterial species. Until now, no report on the application of such composite electrodes in methane production has been published.
The features of cathode materials have distinct effects on the attachment of biofilms and their subsequent electron transport chains. Introducing chemical functional groups on electrodes was found to change the hydrophilic nature of the electrode, making the electrode highly biocompatible for biofilm development [20]. For example, a treatment with aryldiazonium cations on electrodes enhanced the attachment of electrocatalytic biofilms on the electrode surface [21]. In addition, the surface charge of an electrode is also an important factor governing the microbial composition on the electrode surface. The attachment of Geobacter spp. was enhanced on graphite electrodes modified with aminophenyl functional groups due to the enhanced positive charge of the surface [22]. Moreover, the cathodes in MEC-AD systems are expected to have a biofilm consisting of electroactive microbes that are responsible for the autotrophic fixation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to methane. Hydrogen gas can be generated at the cathode via water electrolysis reactions and microbial catalysts, and hydrogenotrophic methanogen communities commonly develop during MEC-AD with different cathode materials [23]. The highest methane yield was reported due to the enhanced growth of hydrogenotrophic methanogens, i. e., Methanobacterium and Methanospirillum [24]. However, in a cathode made with a graphite plate, the acetoclastic methanogen Methanothrix dominated, with an abundance of \(92.7\%\) [25], which might be related to the direct uptake of electrons to reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to methane. Thus, the different electron uptake mechanisms might depend on the cathode materials used during the bioelectromethanation process. However, the types of methanogens that dominate at the MOF-modified cathodes and the involved electron transport chains need to be further explored.
In this work, we designed and developed a novel cathode modified with MOF bimetallic hybrids to evaluate the cathode's methane production performance in an MEC-AD system. For this purpose, a Ni/Co-NC cathode was prepared by coating a Ni/Co-NC catalyst on carbon felt using a polytetrafluoroethylene method. The surface properties and electrochemical performances were characterized with X-ray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM), etc. The effect of the Ni/Co-NC cathode on methane production was investigated in terms of the methane yield, production rate, and biotic current measurements, and the results were compared and discussed. The adhesion of biofilms to the cathodes and their related microbial communities were examined.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Reagents
\(\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) and nickel phthalocyanine were obtained from Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. 2-Methylimidazole was purchased from Aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd. HCl, ethanol, and N,N-dimethylformamide were obtained from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China. The carbon black and polytetrafluoroethylene were purchased from Alfa Aesar and Shanghai Hesen Electric Co., Ltd., respectively. All chemicals for the synthesis of nanomaterials were used as received without further purification.
## 2.2. Catalyst preparation and cathode fabrication
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of ZIF-67 nanocubes
In a typical procedure, \(5.5~\mathrm{g}\) of 2-methylimidazole and \(0.45~\mathrm{g}\) of Co \(\mathrm{(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) were dissolved in \(20\,\mathrm{~mL~}\) and \(3\,\mathrm{~mL~}\) deionized water, respectively, to form solution A and solution B. Then, solution B was added dropwise to solution A under magnetic stirring. The mixture was vigorously stirred at room temperature for \(^{6\,\mathrm{h}}\) . Afterwards, the solid was collected by centrifugation \((8000~\mathrm{rpm}\) , \(15\;\mathrm{min}]\) ) and rinsed with deionized water three times before drying at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) to produce ZIF-67.
## 2.2.2. Synthesis and acid-treatment of the Co-NC nanotubes
The ZIF-67 precursor was carbonized in a covered quartz container at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) at a heating rate of \(2\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow to obtain Co-NC. After that, \(100~\mathrm{mg}\) of the prepared Co-NC was dispersed uniformly into \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) of a \(4\mathrm{~M~}\) HCl solution by ultrasonication for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) and stirred at ambient temperature for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . The sediment was washed with ethanol several times and dried at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) .
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of the Ni/Co-NC catalyst
One hundred milligrams of the acid-treated Co-NC was added to 100 mL N,N-dimethylformamide with the assistance of ultrasonication for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) followed by the addition of \(10\;\mathrm{mg}\) of nickel phthalocyanine and stirring for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . Subsequently, the resulting solid was obtained by centrifugation \((8000\ \ \mathrm{rpm},\ \ \ 10\ \ \ \mathrm{min})\) and washed with N,Ndimethylformamide and ethanol three times. Finally, the black precipitate was dried at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and annealed at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) at a ramp rate of \(5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min}\) under a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere to obtain the Ni/Co-NC catalyst. The simplified synthesis procedure for Ni/Co-NC is shown in Fig. 1.
## 2.2.4. Cathode fabrication
The MEC cathode was fabricated by mounting the Ni/Co-NC catalyst layer on a carbon felt fabric with a surface area of \(16~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) \((4\,\times\,4\,\,\mathrm{cm})\) connected to a titanium wire for external electrical connection. Briefly, \(0.025\ \mathrm{g}\) of the Ni/Co-NC catalyst, \(0.04\ \mathrm{g}\) of carbon black, \(1080~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of ethanol, \(600\,\mathrm{\upmuL}\) of deionized water and \(120\,\mathrm{\upmuL}\) of polytetrafluoroethylene were mixed to form an ink-based catalyst seriflux, which was coated onto the carbon felt after shaking for \(10\;s\) . Then, after drying at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) overnight, the cathode was annealed for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) at \(360\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Ni/Co-NC cathode). A cathode without the Ni/Co-NC coating was also fabricated as a control (carbon cathode). The final cathode was stored at room temperature for further use in the MEC-AD systems.
## 2.3. MEC construction and operation
Three groups of single-chambered MEC reactors with a working volume of \(160~\mathrm{mL}\) were constructed and operated under the same conditions. The MEC system assembled with the Ni/Co-NC cathode was operated as the experimental reactor (MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC). The control reactors (MEC-AD-C) were identical to the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC reactors, with the exception that they had carbon cathodes. An AD served as another control reactor. Carbon fibre brushes with a diameter of \(3\;\mathrm{cm}\) and a length of \(6~\mathrm{{cm}}\) were used as the anode electrodes. The anode
electrodes were immersed in acetone for \(12\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and heated at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) before use. The anode and cathode electrodes were connected to the external circuit by a titanium wire. A fixed external voltage of \(0.6\:\mathrm{V}\) was supplied to the MEC-AD using a direct current power supply. An external resistance of \(10\,\Omega\) was loaded between the two electrodes, and the MEC voltage was recorded every \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\) by a data acquisition system (DAQ6510, Keilney, USA).
The anode was preacclimated in a separate single-chamber MEC system with a \(0.6\mathrm{~V~}\) applied voltage using activated sludge from the Shanghai Bailonggang Sewage Treatment Plant, East China, as the inoculum. The anolyte consisted of \(982.5\;\mathrm{mL}\) phosphate buffer solution buffer solution \((0.31\,\mathrm{\g/L\NH_{4}C l}\) , \(2.452\ \mathrm{g/L}\) \(\mathrm{NaH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , \(4.576~\mathrm{g/L}\) \(\mathrm{Na_{2}H P O_{4}}\) , and \(0.13\,\mathrm{\g/L}\) KCl), \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) trace vitamin solution, \(12.5\,\mathrm{~mL~}\) mineral solution and \(^{1}\,\mathbf{g}\) acetate per L. The anolyte was changed every day until a stable maximum voltage was obtained, which indicated that the anode had been acclimated successfully, and then the enriched anode was transferred into the working MEC-AD reactors. The anaerobic granular sludge for the experiments was inoculated from a semicontinuous reactor after long-term and stable acclimation in our lab with a total solids (TS) level of \(18.5\,\mathrm{g/L}\) and a volatile solids (VS) level of 8.4 \(\mathrm{g/L}\) . The reactors were operated in fed-batch mode for four periods with magnetic stirring at a constant temperature of \(37\pm1\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a water bath. For each cycle, \(0.5\:\mathrm{g}\) sodium acetate served as the carbon source, and it was added to each reactor. The loaded reactors were purged with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas for \(10\;\mathrm{\min}\) to ensure strict anaerobic conditions, and the \(\mathsf{p H}\) was adjusted to 7.0.
## 2.4. Analysis methods
## 2.4.1. Characterization and electrochemical assessments
The structural properties of Ni/Co-NC were analysed by Ultima IV high-resolution XRD (Ultima IV, Rigaku, Japan) with \(\mathrm{Cu}\,\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda\) \(=0.15418\;\mathrm{nm})\) at a generator voltage of \(40\,\mathrm{kV}\) . The nitrogen adsorption isotherms were recorded on a nitrogen adsorption surface area and porosity analyser (ASAP2020 HD88, Micromeritics, USA) at liquid nitrogen temperature (77 K). The morphology and structure of the samples were observed using SEM (Zeiss Gemini 300, Germany) and TEM (FEI TE20, USA). The elemental distributions were analysed by energy dispersive spectroscopy, which was affiliated with TEM. The Raman
spectra were determined by laser confocal Raman microspectroscopy (Renishaw inVia, UK) with a laser excitation of \(532\;\mathrm{nm}\) . XPS (Thermo Scientific K-Alpha, USA) was used to analyse the chemical compositions and elemental valence states of the Ni/Co-NC catalyst. The morphologies of the biofilm on the cathode were also observed under SEM. Before observation, the biocathodes were fixed with \(2.5\%\) glutaraldehyde for \(3\;\mathrm{h}\) . Subsequently, the cathodes were dehydrated with \(30\%\) , \(50\%\) , \(70\%\) and \(100\%\) ethanol solutions for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\) each time.
The electrochemical characteristics of the Ni/Co-NC cathode were evaluated in a standard three electrode system using an electrochemical workstation (CHI 660E, Shanghai Chemhua Instruments Co. Ltd. China) with platinum foil as the counterelectrode and a saturated calomel electrode as the reference electrode in \(50\,\mathrm{mM}\) phosphate buffer solution. A cyclic voltammetry (CV) plot was recorded at a scan rate of \(50\,\mathrm{mV}/\mathrm{s}\) in a potential range from \(-0.4\) to \(0.8\,\mathrm{~V~}\) . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed in the potentiostatic mode over a frequency range of 0.01 to \(100\ \mathrm{kHz}\) . Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was conducted on a VMP-300 (Bio-Logic, France). The scan range was from \(-2.5\) to \(2.5\;\mathrm{V}\) at a scan rate of \(10\,\mathrm{mV/s}\) . All the experiments were conducted at room temperature.
## 2.4.2. Sampling and chemical methods
Biogas was collected using an injection syringe every \(^{12}\,\mathrm{h}\) , and the gas components \(\mathrm{(CH_{4}}\) , \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) ) were measured by gas chromatography (Agilent \(6890\;\mathrm{N}\) , Agilent, USA) equipped with a thermal conductivity detector.
At the end of the experiments, the liquid samples were centrifuged at \(10000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) , and then supernatants were filtered through 0.45 \(\upmu\mathrm{m}\) pore-diameter polymer membranes followed by dilution to a feasible range for analysis with a COD-M kit \(50{-}1500\,\mathrm{mg/L}\) (HACH, America) for soluble chemical oxygen demand (SCOD) measurement. The zeta potentials of the cathodes were measured by a Zetasizer Nano (Malvern, UK). The TS and VS of the sludges were analysed according to standard methods [26].
## 2.4.3. Microbial community analysis
At the end of the experiments, the solid samples in the systems were collected by centrifugation to analyse for microbial diversity. DNA extraction was completed at the Shanghai Majorbio Biopharm
Technology Co., Ltd. using E.Z.N.A. \(\textsuperscript{\textregistered}\) Mag-Bind Soil DNA Kits (Omega Biotek, Norcross, GA, USA). The 16S rRNA gene was analysed by primers 338F-806R for bacteria and 524F-958R for archaea. The resulting amplicon libraries were used for quantification by agarose gel electrophoresis and a QuantiFluorTM-ST fluorometer (Promega, USA). An Illumina MiSeq system (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA) was used to sequence the PCR products. The detailed processing methods were described by Amato et al. [27].
Paired-end reads were obtained by Illumina-MiSeq sequencing; therefore, paired-end reads were assembled by PEAR (v0.9.6) first according to the overlap relationships between paired-end reads along with quality filtering. Effective sequences were ultimately produced by cutting off the barcode and primer sequences. The operational taxonomic units (OTUs) were clustered by sequence similarities. Generally, sequences with \(\geq97\%\) nucleotide similarities belong to the same OTUs. Each OTU was annotated using taxonomic information using the Ribosomal Database Project classifier algorithm. The community diversity analyses of the samples were compared at the phylum and genus levels.
## 2.5. Statistical analysis
The bacterial area density on the cathodes was calculated based on SEM according to the following equation [28]:
\[
\rho=10^{6}\times N/A
\]
where \(\uprho\) is the bacterial area density \((\mathrm{cells}/\mathrm{mm}^{2})\) ; N is the number of bacterial cells shown in the corresponding SEM images; and A is the area shown in the corresponding SEM images estimated using the scale bars \((\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2})\) .
The maximum methane production potential and rate were estimated by fitting the experimental data with a modified Gompertz model (Eq. (2)) [29].
\[
y(t)=P\cdot\exp\Big\{-\exp\Big[\frac{\mu_{m}e}{P}(\lambda-t)+1\Big]\Big\}
\]
where t is the digestion time (h), y is the cumulative methane production at time t (mL); P represents the maximum methane production potential (mL); e is a mathematical constant (2.718282); \(\upmu_{\mathrm{m}}\) is the maximum \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) production rate \((\mathrm{mL/d})\) ; and \(\uplambda\) is the lag-phase period (h). Kinetics were determined using a OriginPro 9.1 Software and the coefficient of determination \((\mathbf{R}^{2})\) was used to estimate the prediction accuracy.
The coulombic efficiency (CE) was calculated via Eq. (3). The total methane recovery \(\left(\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{tot}}\right)\) was defined as the ratio of the actual methane recovered and maximum theoretical production based on substrate consumed [30], which was calculated with Eq. (4). The energy recovery efficiency \((\eta)\) , the ratio of the energy utilized during methane production to the sum of the added electric energy and the chemical energy of the matrix, was used to evaluate the energy consumption of the system (Eq. (5)):
\[
\mathrm{CE}(\%)=\frac{M_{\mathrm{AC}}\int_0^tIdt}{n\cdot F\cdot V\cdot\Delta SCOD}\times100\%
\]
\[
R_{tot}(\%)=\frac{V_{CH4}}{0.35V\Delta SCOD}
\]
\[
\eta(\%)=\frac{n_{CH4}\Delta H_{CH_4}}{\frac{\Delta SCOD}{64}\times\Delta H_s+\int\frac{U/-P_{RH}}{1000}\times100\%}
\]
where \(\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{Ac}}\) is the molar weight of acetate \(\left(60\,\mathrm{g/mol}\right)\) ; I is the current (A), which was calculated according to Ohm's law \(\mathrm{(I=V/R)}\) ; t is time (s); n represents the moles of electrons required to oxidize 1 mol of COD; F is the Faraday constant \((964850/\mathrm{mol})\) ; V is the working volume of the reactor (L) and \(\Delta\mathbf{S}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{D}\) is the change in COD during the experiment \(\left(\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{\Lambda}\right)\) ; \(\mathrm{V_{CH4}}\) is the volume of produced methane; \(\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{CH4}}\) is the moles of produced methane; \(\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{CH4}}\) and \(\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{S}}\) are the molar Gibbs free energy for combustion of methane \(\left(890\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\right)\) and acetate \((870.28\,\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) ; U is the applied voltage (V); and R is the resistance \((\Omega)\) .
combustion of methane \(\left(890\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\right)\) and acetate \((870.28\,\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) ; U is the applied voltage (V); and R is the resistance \((\Omega)\) .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characteristics of the electrode materials
The XRD patterns for ZIF-67, Co-NC and Ni/Co-NC are illustrated in Fig. 2a. ZIF-67 exhibited its main diffraction peaks at approximately \(2\theta\) \(=10.4^{\circ}\) , \(12.7^{\circ}\) , \(14.6^{\circ}\) , \(16.5^{\circ}\) , \(18.1^{\circ}\) , \(24.6^{\circ}\) , \(26.7^{\circ}\) , and \(32.4^{\circ}\) . The results were in accordance with the peaks for the ZIF-67 (0 0 2), (1 1 2), (0 2 2), (0 1 3), (2 2 2), (2 3 3), (1 3 4), (0 4 4) and (2 3 5) planes of the ZIF-67 crystal (PDF card No. 43–0144) [31]. After annealing at \(900\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , the ZIF-67 precursor was converted into Co-NC. Three clear characteristic diffraction peaks at \(2\theta=44.2^{\circ}\) , \(51.5^{\circ}\) and \(75.9^{\circ}\) were observed in the XRD pattern for Co-NC, which were attributed to the (1 1 1), (2 0 0) and (2 2 0) planes of the face-centred cubic Co (JCPDS Card No. 15–0806) [32]. The electrode material Ni/Co-NC was obtained by HCl etching and doping with nickel phthalocyanine. A diffraction peak at \(2\theta=25^{\circ}\) was observed, corresponding to the cubic phase of the peak of C (0 0 2) [33]. The peak intensity of Co decreased compared with that of Co-NC, which may be attributed to the dissolution of some portion of the bare metal Co particles after \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) of HCl etching, thereby exposing more carbon. Diffraction peaks resulting from impurities were not detected in the XRD curve of Ni/Co-NC, indicating that the electrode material was successfully synthesized.
Fig. 2b shows the \(\mathbf{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption curve measurements of ZIF-67 and Ni/Co-NC. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) equation and Jarrett–Joyner–Halenda model were used to calculate the specific surface area and pore size, respectively. The BET surface area of Ni/Co-NC was \(254.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (Table S1), and the value was noticeably higher than that of \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) (ca. \(180\sim190~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) [34,35]. More active sites for electrocatalysis can be provided with a larger surface area. In addition to surface area optimization, porosity and pore size are also crucial in making electrode materials [36]. As shown in Fig. 2b, the curve for Ni/ Co-NC displayed a hysteresis loop in the \(\mathrm{P/P_{0}}\) range of 0.43–0.99, and the value indicated the formation of microporous and mesoporous structures in the material [33]. The average pore sizes of ZIF-67 and Ni/ Co-NC were 14.8 and \(43.2\;\mathrm{nm}\) , respectively (Table S1). Compared with ZIF-67, the decreased surface area of Ni/Co-NC implied the production of larger pores with annealing and with the introduction of Ni.
electrocatalytic process [39].
The morphology of the Ni/Co-NC catalyst was further characterized by TEM and HR-TEM images. As shown in Fig. 3a-b, the Ni/Co-NC consisted of homogeneously dispersed nanoparticles with a uniform size of approximately \(18\;\mathrm{nm}\), and a small quantity of carbon nanotubes was clearly visible on the surface. In addition, characteristic lattice fringes could be identified from the HR-TEM images (Fig. 3c). An interplanar distance of \(\approx\,0.203\,\mathrm{~nm}\) was found with HR-TEM with distinct spots shown in the corresponding fast Fourier transform image (inset of Fig. 3c), which is between the (111) plane spacing of the Co nanoparticles and the Ni nanoparticles. This result indicated that a Co-Ni bimetallic alloy was formed [32]. In addition, the nanoparticles were surrounded by N-doped graphene carbon layers with an interplanar distance of \(0.344\ \mathrm{nm}\), which was assigned to the (002) planes of the carbon. This conclusion agreed well with XRD results. Moreover, the corresponding energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mappings of C (yellow), N (blue), Co (red) and Ni (green) in Ni/Co-NC are depicted in Fig. 3d-g, revealing a homogeneous distribution for C, N, Co and Ni. This confirmed the successful formation of bimetallic Ni/Co-NC rather than the formation of a physically mixed material.
To further understand the microstructure of the electrode material, SEM images were recorded. Compared with the polyhedrons and smooth surfaces of ZIF-67, Ni/Co-NC retained the average particle size but exhibited a hollow structure and rough surfaces (Fig. S1a-b). The rough surface was due to the formation of Ni nanoparticles and carbon nanotubes formed by the carbonization of organic ligands. The hollow structure was found to be beneficial for electron transfer enhancement [40]. Moreover, the surfaces of the pristine carbon felt and the Ni/Co-NC cathode were also observed by SEM, as shown in Fig. S1c-d. A large quantity of particles and clusters appeared on the newly synthesized Ni/ Co-NC cathode, and the surface became rougher. The above surficial characteristics of the electrode were expected to provide more catalytic sites for hydrogen evolution, and further information is explored in the following sections.
## 3.2. Electrochemical performance of the cathode
The electrochemical performance of the Ni/Co-NC and carbon cathodes were investigated in a three-electrode system. CV curves were applied at a scan rate of \(50\;\mathrm{mV/s}\) to evaluate the capacitive performance
of the cathodes. As shown in Fig. 3h, the Ni/Co-NC cathode exhibited a large CV shape area, and the calculated specific capacitance was \(657.05\mathrm{F/m^{2}}\), approximately 3 times larger than the value \(203.49\mathrm{F/m^{2}}\) for the carbon cathode. Moreover, the Ni/Co-NC catalyst significantly increased the current response of the electrode. The Ni/Co-NC cathode obtained a higher nonfaradaic current of \(14.85\,\mathrm{mA}\) than the carbon cathode with a measured value of \(3.06\;\mathrm{mA}\). The increase in nonfaradaic current could promote mass transfer from the electrolyte to the cathode surface.
EIS Nyquist was carried out to further analyse the interfacial charge transport resistance of the cathodes, and the data were simulated and fitted using an equivalent circuit (Fig. 3i). \(\mathrm{R_{s}}\) represents the solution resistance, while \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) indicates the resistance to the charge transfer at the electrode surface. The diameter of the semicircle on the EIS Nyquist plots of the Ni/Co-NC cathode was smaller than that of the carbon cathode. The carbon cathode had a charge transfer resistance of \(247.6\,\Omega\). In the presence of the Ni/Co-NC catalyst, the charge transfer resistance
of the cathode drastically decreased to \(2.51\,\Omega\). This result was in accordance with the CV analysis, demonstrating that the charge transfer resistance should be reduced on the Ni/Co-NC cathode, accelerating electron transfer to microorganisms.
Moreover, the LSV results showed that the Ni/Co-NC cathode had a higher hydrogen evolution reaction activity with lower overpotentials of \(226\;\mathrm{mV}\) at a current density of \(10\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) than the carbon cathode (274 \(\mathrm{mV}\)). This result indicated that more endogenous hydrogen could be released by the Ni/Co-NC cathode, which might be beneficial for hydrogenotrophic methanogenesis (HM) during the MEC-AD process.
## 3.3. Methane production and current generation
The prepared Ni/Co-NC cathode was used in a single-chambered MEC to investigate its effect on methane generation, and the performance was compared with MEC-AD-C and AD. The biogas accumulation and composition were measured at \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) intervals. As shown in Fig. 4a,
methane accumulation increased rapidly during the first \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) and reached \(128.8\pm1.6\:\mathrm{mL}\) and \(114.3\pm2.1\:\mathrm{mL}\) in MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC and MEC-AD-C processes at the end of the experiment, which were approximately 3.9 and 3.5 times higher than that of AD \((32.9\ \mathrm{mL})\) , respectively. Methane percentages of ca. \(90\%\) were obtained in the MECAD reactors, which might be attributed to the higher methane production in those reactors (Fig. 4b). Meanwhile, MEC-AD with the Ni/Co-NC cathode and the carbon cathode obtained SCOD removal efficiencies of \(87\%\) and \(85\%\) , respectively, which were higher than that of AD \((74\%)\) , as shown in Fig. 4c. In addition, MEC-AD effectively consumed low concentrations of acetate during the latter period of the reaction. The applied voltage during MEC-AD can improve COD degradation and thereby lead to higher methane production.
The results of the \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{tot}}\) revealed the Ni/Co-NC cathode enhanced the methane recovery by \(11\%\) in comparison to that of the carbon cathode (Fig. 4d). These indicated Ni/Co-NC cathode showed higher rate of diffusion of electron at the cathode surface, which was in accordance
with the EIS analysis. CE was measured to evaluate the percent of possible electrons transferred to the external circuit from the substrate removed [41]. As depicted in Fig. 4d, the CE of the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC process was higher \((78.2\%)\) than that of the MEC-AD-C process \((71.7\%)\) . This may be attributed to the presence of Ni/Co-NC catalyst on the cathode surface, which augmented the biocompatibility of cathode, thereby improving the redox reactions with microbes in MEC-AD [42]. Furthermore, the energy recovery efficiency \(\boldsymbol{\upeta}\) was increased from \(73\%\) in the MEC-AD-C reactor to \(81\%\) in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC reactor. Apparently, using Ni/Co-NC as cathode catalyst benefited the MEC-AD from the perspective of methane recovery with applied voltage.
The highest methane yield of \(0.34\;\mathrm{m}^{3}\,\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{COD}\) was achieved in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC reactor, indicating a \(13\%\) increase in comparison to that in the MEC-AD-C reactor. To our knowledge, the methane yield was comparatively higher than those reported in MEC-AD systems with different catalyst-modified cathodes in lab-scale reactors (Table 2). For example, a previous study reported that a carbon felt cathode could only
reach a methane yield of \(0.04 \sim 0.09 \, \mathrm{m}^{3} \, \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kgCOD}\) under similar conditions [9; 43]. Similarly, a methane yield of only \(0.08 \sim 0.33 \, \mathrm{m}^{3} \, \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kgCOD}\) was achieved with a Pt-modified cathode [6,44,45]. Nickel mesh and nickel foam were also used as cathodes for MEC-AD with a 0.8 V external voltage, attaining methane yields of \(0.14 \, \mathrm{m}^{3} \, \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kgCOD}\) [45] and \(0.196 \, \mathrm{m}^{3} \, \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kgCOD}\) [46], respectively. The methane yield of the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC process was also higher than the values of other advanced nanomaterial-modified cathodes possessing high surface areas and conductivities, such as \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) \((0.27 \, \mathrm{m}^{3} \, \mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kgCOD})\) [1].
The methane production data showed a good fit with the modified Gompertz model \((\mathrm{R}^{2} > 0.99)\), and the key parameters are summarized in Table 1. In addition to increasing the methane potential for MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC, the maximum methane production rate in this reactor was calculated to be \(0.57 \, \mathrm{m}^{3}/(\mathrm{m}^{3} \cdot \mathrm{d})\), which was 0.5 times and 8.5 times higher than that in the MEC-AD-C and AD processes, respectively. Meanwhile, the maximum methane production rates obtained were significantly enhanced in comparison with those of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) cathodes (0.1 \(\mathrm{m}^{3}/(\mathrm{m}^{3} \cdot \mathrm{d})\) [44] and nickel mesh cathodes \((0.367 \, \mathrm{m}^{3}/(\mathrm{m}^{3} \cdot \mathrm{d})\) [45]. For the lag phase time \((\lambda)\), the Ni/Co-NC cathode shortened the methane production initiation time (from \(4.6 \pm 1.4 \, \mathrm{h}\) in MEC-AD and \(36.3 \pm 0.7 \, \mathrm{h}\) in AD to \(2.0 \pm 1.1 \, \mathrm{h}\) in MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC). The surface charge test showed that the Ni/Co-NC cathode was positively charged, while the carbon cathode possessed a negative surface charge (Table S2). Thus, the electrostatic attraction force between the cathode and the microorganisms might be strengthened on the Ni/Co-NC cathode. Moreover, the observed high specific surface area and the high electrical conductivity of the Ni/Co-NC cathode could provide a platform for the greater colonization of microorganisms. In view of this, it may be speculated that the enhancement of the methane accumulation and production rates were linked to higher microbial activities during the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC process.
The electron uptake rate of methanogens from the cathode to reduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) can be indicated by the electric current [47]. The currents of the Ni/Co-NC cathode and carbon cathode were further recorded after three batch feeding cycles, and the current responses are shown in Fig. 4e. The initial fluctuation was caused by the replacement of the medium. The maximum current in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC reactor (11.4 mA) was more prominent than that in the MEC-AD-C reactor \((10.2 \, \mathrm{mA})\). Under similar MEC-AD configuration conditions, the maximum value of the Pt/carbon cloth cathode was only \(8.6 \, \mathrm{mA}\) [45]. The higher maximum current was associated with the accelerated electron exchange efficiency between the microorganisms and the electrode, thus enhancing the methane yield. The current then remained stable, with the formation of mature electroactive biofilms on the electrodes. Corresponding with the complete consumption of substrates after \(60 \, \mathrm{h}\), the electron transfer between the cathode and the electroactive methanogens decreased, and the current gradually decreased.
## 3.4. Development of biofilms on the cathode and the microbial community
## 3.4.1. Microbial community
The microbial communities in the sludge obtained from the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC, MEC-AD-C and AD reactors were characterized after four cycles of operation. Compared with MEC-AD-C and AD, the Simpson diversity index and Shannon diversity index were slightly lower in the sludge of the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC reactor, suggesting that the Ni/Co-NC cathode can probably develop a microenvironment that enriches host
specific communities (Table S3). Similarly, Flores-Rodriguez et al. [48] also found lower community diversity values on biocathodes than during AD.
To gain insights into the difference between the microbial communities, the relative bacterial community abundance was identified. Synergistota, Actinobacteriota, Firmicutes, Chloroflexi and Bacteroidota were the main bacterial communities in all the sludge samples (Fig. 5a). All of these phyla were reported to be common bacteria in the AD system. Among them, Synergistota was the dominant phylum in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC system that played an important role in anaerobic methane production [49]. Previous studies have reported that Synergistota contain abundant organic matter-degrading functional microorganisms [50,51]. As shown in Fig. 5b, at the genus level, electrical stimulation increased the abundance of Thermovirga, especially in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC system. It has also been shown that Thermovirga has a positive response to anaerobic processes that are augmented with bioelectrochemical activity via conductive material addition or voltage application, where they participate in direct interspecific electron transfer [52]. In addition, Thermovirga was beneficial for COD degradation, which was also revealed to be the phylotype of syntrophic bacteria in methanogenic enrichments [53]. Therefore, these results confirmed our previous hypothesis that the colonization by electroactive microorganisms facilitated the COD degradation and methane production on the Ni/Co-NC cathode.
Regarding the archaeal community at the genus level, Methanobacterium, Methanosaeta and Methanosarcina were the main species in the three reactors (Fig. 5c). In AD, Methanosaeta was the most abundant methanogen, accounting for \(51.2\%\). However, Methanobacterium dominated with \(62.4\%\) in the MEC-AD-C process and \(79.6\%\) in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC process, followed by acetoclastic methanogens Methanosaeta (obligate) and Methanosarcina (facultative). This result was consistent with the findings by Xu et al. [54], where Methanobacterium constituted \(66.9\%\) of a MEC-AD system with carbon cloth as the cathode. The significant enrichment of Methanobacterium, especially that induced by the Ni/Co-NC cathode, indicated that the methanogenic metabolism in the MEC-AD reactors shifted from acetoclastic methanogenesis to HM. The enrichment of Methanobacterium was beneficial for endogenous hydrogen bioconversion to methane, which was in agreement with the exceptional hydrogen evolution activity of the Ni/Co-NC cathode (as discussed in Section 3.2). Similarly, any hydrogen that permeated from the surface of the Pt cloth cathode was quickly utilized by the hydrogenotrophic methanogens [55].
Interestingly, Methanosaeta harundinacea 6Ac, a species of Methanosaeta, was identified in the two MEC-AD reactors tested in this study. Methanosaeta harundinacea can accept electrons via direct electron transfer (DET) for the reduction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to methane [56]. Moreover, Methanobacterium possesses electrochemical activity and is capable of directly accepting electrons from extracellular electron transfer components, e.g., Geobacter or electrodes [57]. Therefore, it is speculated that the Ni/Co-NC cathode is very likely to boost methane production by simultaneously strengthening the HM and the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-dependent methanogenesis pathway (DET pathway). Further studies are still required to fully understand the contribution of these two metabolic methanation pathways.
## 3.4.2. SEM of the biofilm on the Ni/Co-NC cathode
SEM images were recorded to visualize the microbial biofilm growth on the cathodes after repeated cycles. As shown in Fig. 6a-b, many rod-shaped microbes were firmly associated with the surface of the Ni/Co-NC cathode, and they nearly covered the entire electrode to form a thick biofilm. While there were fewer rod-shaped microbes, coccishaped microbes were observed on the carbon cathode (Fig. 6c). The different morphologies of the microbes revealed differences in the microbial communities on the two tested cathodes. According to our previous study, the rod-shaped microbe was Methanobacterium. The microbial area densities at the cathodes of the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC and
MEC-AD-C reactors were calculated to be 208,333 cells/mm² and 96,450 cells/mm², respectively. The Ni/Co-NC cathode provided anchorage points for hydrogenotrophic methanogen attachment, mainly Methanobacterium, to consume the evolved hydrogen from the cathode.
Interestingly, numerous bacterial nanowires (or pili) existed on the surface of the cells on the Ni/Co-NC cathode, as seen at high magnification (Fig. 6b). These might serve as conductive bridges that accelerate electron transfer from cell to cell and from cell to electrode. Efficient extracellular electron transfer between the bacteria and electrodes could facilitate biofilm formation, consistent with the EIS analysis in this study. Notably, some gel-like components were observed (Fig. 6a), which were likely the extracellular polymeric substances of the microorganisms. All the above results further demonstrate that the biocompatibility of the Ni/Co-NC cathode can facilitate the accumulation of biofilms of hydrogenotrophic Methanobacterium and play an important role in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) biomethanation.
## 3.5. Methane production pathway in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC system
Based on the above discussion, potential metabolic pathways for methane production in the MEC-AD-Ni/Co-NC system were proposed (Fig. 7). In the MEC-AD system, organic matter is known to be oxidized into electrons, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}\)via anode oxidation. The electrons produced at the anode are transferred exogenously to the cathode through the external circuit, while \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}\)flow to the cathode through the liquid phase for methane production. The experimental results indicated that the MOF-derived Ni/Co-NC cathode was more beneficial than the carbon cathode in terms of methane production and organic matter degradation. The enhanced performance of the MEC-AD process with the Ni/Co-NC cathode was primarily due to four reasons. First, Ni/Co
NC cathode with excellent hydrogen evolution activity could release more endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and then select or enrich more functional microbes of HM (Methanobacterium) than carbon cathode, so as to promote HM pathway. Second, compared with carbon cathode, the more electrically conductive Ni/Co-NC cathode could strengthen DET between cathode and methanogens, such as Methanobacterium and Methanosaeta harundinacea 6Ac, which accelerated the DET pathway. Several previous studies with other cathodes have also reported that the electrode improved methane production and the degradation rate of volatile organic acids by DET stimulation in MEC-AD systems [57,58]. Third, Ni/ Co-NC cathode provided abundant space for the growth and attachment of electroactive microorganisms due to its large surface area, positive charge and rough surface. Attached growth tended to maintain more electroactive microbes and promote the interactions between cathode and electroactive microorganisms. This contributed to both HM and DET pathways. Last but not least, Ni/Co-NC cathode was shown to have negative on the growth of acetoclastic methanogens indicating the acetoclastic methanogenesis pathway was inhibited. In conclusion, the introduction of Ni/Co-NC cathode could shift methanogenic metabolism from acetoclastic methanogenesis to HM and further enhance methane production by accelerating HM and DET pathways, which were thermodynamically favourable.
## 4. Conclusion
This study investigated the performance of a novel MOF-derived Ni/ Co-NC cathode for potential applications in methane production in MEC-AD systems. The highest methane yield of \(0.34\,\mathrm{m}^{3}\,\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{COD}\) and methane production rate of \(0.57\,\mathrm{m}^{3}/(\mathrm{m}^{3}.\mathrm{d})\) were achieved during MECAD operation with a Ni/Co-NC cathode, which were \(13\%\) and \(50\%\)
higher than those with a carbon cathode. Meanwhile, the Ni/Co-NC cathode also improved the SCOD removal rate \((87\%)\) and current generation. The CV and EIS analyses demonstrated that Ni/Co-NC notably increased the nonfaradaic current and reduced the charge transfer resistance on the surface of the cathode. In addition, the reduced overpotential of the Ni/Co-NC cathode facilitated hydrogen evolution, resulting in the enrichment of Methanobacterium, which further enhanced hydrogenotrophic methanogenesis. The appearance of Methanosaeta harundinacea 6Ac species indicated Ni/Co-NC cathode may accelerate direct electron transfer pathway to enhance methane production. Therefore, the Ni/Co-NC cathode is a promising and cost-effective alternative for methane generation for future MEC-AD systems.
